A||||9||ayamnik||s15 4.3L in a fiero ||||5
Z||000000||ayamnik||09-23-1999||01:45 PM||||I have a 1990 GMC s15 PU with a 4.3 in it the motor runs better than new but I cracked the frame mudding. I like to put it in my Fiero. Will the engine mounts bolt up. Will the trany from a Fiero v6 bolt up<BR>If not where can I get a kit to make the trany fit. Are there any other traneys out there that I can use that will fit. Any web pages I can look at? I just cant get my fiero motor running god it still idles at 1500-900-200-1500. Any one got a 5 speed for sale thet will fit? <BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||09-23-1999||02:32 PM||||A 4.3 engine will not fit any Fiero tranny. You need to get a V8 adaptor plate and some other V8 swap kit parts. Looking at V8 Archie's website will give you an idea what to expect. The good thing is that you don't have to cut a hole into the inner fender well with a 4.3 like you have to with a V8. It fits better and is lighter than a small block Chevy 350.||||reg||1
Z||000002||88 Fiero||09-23-1999||03:19 PM||||Hey Bat...Do you have the Archie kit on your Fiero??? Reason is I've seen the kit and you really have to cut on the wall of the car...The Gary Zumalt kit for some reason you don't have to...I think I would look at both kits and compare it to one another...This is very interesting subject...||||reg||1
Z||000003||Richard Parnell||09-23-1999||04:34 PM||||88 Fiero- I think the main difference between the V8 archie kit and Zumwalt is that the archie kit doesnt need to cut the left axle or relocate transmission mounting.  The archie kit does go into the wheel well but the cut is not a structural one.  I may be wrong on my info, but that's what I've gleaned in my rummaging around different sites.Anyone?||||reg||1
Z||000004||ayamnik||09-23-1999||05:51 PM||||Is there a difference between a 4cyl and v6 trany one should I use. Can you point me to the web where I can look at different kit’s to install my 4.3? ||||reg||5
Z||000005||VISCERAL||09-23-1999||07:14 PM||||Hey, if nothing works out I'll buy the 4.3 from you||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||09-23-1999||09:03 PM||||Yes, the swap kit for my 350 in the Batmobile is a V8 Archie's. Richard is right on the money with his discription. The V8 Archie's engine crowds the right side of the engine compartment and needs a hole cut in the inner fender well. The Zumalt kit centers the engine more in the compartment which requires relocating the tranny, using different length axles, and cutting a hunk out of the frame.<P>The 4.3 bellhousing bolts up to anything a Chevy small block V8 does. I don't know of any 4.3 swap kits for a Fiero, so you must use a Chevy V8 conversion kit in order to install it into a Fiero. There are some things in the kit that you don't need for a 4.3, for example, the special water pump pulleys and belt. Here is V8 Archie's website:<P> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||mwbackus||09-24-1999||02:43 AM||||One possible thing I would alter on the 4.3 install vs. the small block is the adapter plate.  There are aluminum adapter plates available from Street Dreams ( <A HREF="http://www.iserv.net/~strdream/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.iserv.net/~strdream/</A>  )that may be a more logical approach.  This would help somewhat as far as power to weight optimization is concerned, which is one reasons for my decision to use the turbo 4.3 over the small block. Obviously, the mounts, flywheel, clutch and other swap kit items are best purchased from V8archie.  One area that does present quite a challenge, is the relocation of the alternator on the later model 4.3's with the single serpentine belt drive.  If air conditioning is to be retained, extensive fabrication of brackets and pulleys to allow this will be required.  Since the 4.3 is much shorter than the small block, the front engine mount bolts end up in a less than desirable location (ie..air).  One should keep in mind that this is by no means a plug and play installation.  At the very least, you will need to spend the equivalent (if not more) of a V8 swap by the time it is completed.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||09-24-1999||04:29 PM||||MW, you're right about that Street Dreams aluminum adaptor plate being a low cost, low weight alternative. My 87 SE Fiero with a dead 2.5  motor is begging for a freshly rebuilt 4.3 V6. My dad has a complete 1988 4.3 engine sitting in his shed that I can use too. Wish I didn't already have too many projects going on at once right now.||||reg||1
Z||000009||mwbackus||09-24-1999||10:00 PM||||Batboy: You may also find the Syclone/Typhone site quite interesting. <A HREF="http://www.syty.org/syty.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.syty.org/syty.html</A>  It seems that one of the big things with these owners is the desire to remove the stock turbos, intercooler, manifold, etc. and replace them with higher performance units.  This provides for a good source of these slightly used parts at bargain prices. :-)  I was considering the idea of rebuilding a NA 4.3 Vortec to turbo specs and obtaining the turbo setup etc. from this channel.  It would have been much cheaper for me to do this rather than purchasing a complete turbo engine.  Oh well.  <P>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
